    "podcasts": [
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "santos-de-cartier",
        "slug": "santos-de-cartier",
        "order": "00",
        "title": "Santos de Cartier",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This Santos de Cartier timepiece houses a\n chronograph movement capable of measuring\n short times. Look closely at the dial and you’ll see\n second, minute, and hour counters, in addition to\n the current time. The calibre integrates two push\n buttons, one to stop and start the timer and\n another to reset it. This functionality is possible\n thanks to the talent of Cartier’s watchmakers, who\n meticulously developed the automatic mechanical\n movement called the 1904 MC Chronograph, in\n honour of the year that the Santos de Cartier was\n first created.\n These components are assembled precisely,\n respecting the complexity belied by the clean and\n precise proportions of this square shaped icon.",
        "image": {
          "name": "santos-de-cartier-1"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "santos-de-cartier-1_mask.png",
          "width": 591,
          "height": 780
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_1.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "santos-dumont",
        "slug": "santos-dumont",
        "order": "02",
        "title": "Santos-Dumont",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This watchmaking creation celebrates the subtle\n beauty of a stone dial. Look closely, and you can\n see it’s made from golden obsidian. Tiny trapped\n bubbles in the volcanic stone are responsible for\n the iridescent reflections. The obsidian is cut to fit\n precisely into the Santos-Dumont case, just\n 0.3 mm thick, and polished carefully to release its\n sparkle. Each one is a product of nature and truly\n unique.\n This marks a new reimagining of materials in the\n Santos-Dumont collection.",
        "image": {
          "name": "santos-dumont-1"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "santos-dumont-1_mask.png",
          "width": 540,
          "height": 540
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_2.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "santos-dumont-2",
        "slug": "santos-dumont-2",
        "order": 10,
        "title": "Santos-Dumont",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This Santos-Dumont watch showcases a new\n bracelet, developed in the Cartier Manufacture. This\n type of metal mesh is made up of tiny links that are\n just 1.15 mm thick. Each bracelet is composed of 15 rows, with a total of 394 links.\n The links are manufactured and finished separately,\n because each of these elements must adhere to an accuracy of 1/100th of a millimetre. The Maison’s\n bracelet experts then take over: they manually assemble the links one by one to create the finished\n piece, ensuring an elegant and fluid movement that\n hugs the wrist.",
        "image": {
          "name": "santos-dumont-2"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "santos-dumont-2_mask.png",
          "width": 540,
          "height": 540
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_3.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "santos-dumont-3",
        "slug": "santos-dumont-3",
        "order": 13,
        "title": "Santos-Dumont",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "The Santos-Dumont is a true icon in the world of\n watchmaking. It was revolutionary in its time\n because it was the first watch specifically designed\n to be worn on the wrist, rather than kept on a chain,\n in a pocket.\n This latest version plays with the legacy of that\n bracelet and takes inspiration from vintage Cartier\n designs.\n In the archives, the Maison’s designers unearthed\n special-order bracelets with very fine, fluid links.\n Working with the bracelet-making experts at the\n manufacture, they developed an ergonomic new\n construction to keep those pure lines and\n accentuate the sensorial nature of its flexibility",
        "image": {
          "name": "santos-de-cartier-2"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "santos-de-cartier-2_mask.png",
          "width": 540,
          "height": 540
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_4.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "roadster",
        "slug": "roadster",
        "order": "01",
        "title": "Roadster",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "The Maison called on over 100 areas of expertise\n to bring back the Roadster. Here, we would like to\n draw your attention to the dial, which presents a\n decorative final touch.\n The appliqué on the face of the Roadster integrates\n an interplay of textures and depths, as well as\n different finishes, truly bringing the watch to life\n through meticulous detail and subtle balance.",
        "image": {
          "name": "roadster-1"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "roadster-1_mask.png",
          "width": 600,
          "height": 660
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_5.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "roadster-2",
        "slug": "roadster-2",
        "order": "04",
        "title": "Roadster",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "When it was first introduced, the Roadster made\n a bold choice to take codes from the world of\n mechanical fuselages.\n The designers’ approach to the new Roadster\n was to take that legacy and refine it. You can see\n the inspiration of a speed counter, and a shaped\n winding crown that feels almost automative, like\n a headlight. Then of course there are the visible\n screws, which are a signature of Cartier design,\n from the very first Santos de Cartier to the iconic\n LOVE bracelet and now of course, the Roadster.",
        "image": {
          "name": "roadster-2"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "roadster-2_mask.png",
          "width": 600,
          "height": 660
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_6.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "cartier-prive",
        "slug": "cartier-prive",
        "order": 12,
        "title": "Cartier Privé",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This trio of watches pays homage to platinum, one\n of Cartier’s signature materials, in watchmaking just\n as much as in jewellery. As a testament to the\n watchmaker of shapes, the artisan forms the raw\n platinum into the instantly recognisable silhouettes\n of the Tortue, Crash Skeleton, and Tank Normale.\n At Cartier, platinum is linked to the ruby and its\n nuanced red colour. Here, the magnificent colour runs\n through the collection, offering burgundy straps\n and dial decals in the same tone. This is the 10th\n Cartier Privé Opus, and it marks the return of these\n three emblematic, highly sought-after models.",
        "image": {
          "name": "cartier-prive"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "cartier-prive_mask.png",
          "width": 540,
          "height": 540
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_7.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "tank-americaine",
        "slug": "tank-americaine",
        "order": "02",
        "title": "Tank Américaine",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This is the first time the Maison has equipped the\n Tank Américaine with a skeleton movement. The\n watchmakers took on a real technical and aesthetic\n challenge to find something that would do justice to\n this iconic shape, which has a cult following.\n Designers and watchmakers worked together to\n come up with the 1988 MC, named after the release\n year of the first Tank Américaine. Its linear\n architecture gives a striking level of transparency,\n and these minimal lines showcase, and even\n accentuate, the shape of Tank Américaine.",
        "image": {
          "name": "tank-americaine-1"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "tank-americaine-1_mask.png",
          "width": 537,
          "height": 780
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_8.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "tank-americaine-2",
        "slug": "tank-americaine-2",
        "order": 8,
        "title": "Tank Américaine",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "What is fascinating about this new creation is the\n geometry at the heart of its design. This is a real\n signature of Cartier style, and for this first\n skeleton version, the Maison wanted to pay\n tribute to the importance of Art Deco in its design\n heritage.\n The open simplicity of the shapes frames the\n complexity of the movement and the level of\n detail. Look closely at the engraved railway\n minute track, and the rose gold bridge on the two-\n tone version.",
        "image": {
          "name": "tank-americaine-2"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "tank-americaine-2_mask.png",
          "width": 537,
          "height": 780
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_9.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "tortue",
        "slug": "tortue",
        "order": 9,
        "title": "Tortue",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This Tortue watch pays homage to the technical\n excellence of the Maison’s gem setters, bringing\n watchmaking and jewellery expertise together.\n The setters use different diamond setting\n techniques to maximise the refractions of light\n and ensure that this watch sparkles infinitely. On\n the sides of the watch, note the setting technique\n called 'serti descendu'. This savoir-faire reduces\n the metal to a minimum, so the silhouette is\n defined by the diamonds.",
        "image": {
          "name": "tortue"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "tortue_mask.png",
          "width": 600,
          "height": 660
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_10.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "baignoire",
        "slug": "baignoire",
        "order": "06",
        "title": "Baignoire",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "This exceptional version of the Baignoire watch is\n a manifesto in precious stones. 100 brilliant-cut\n diamonds are snow-set on the dial\n alone. On the case and the bangle, there are\n another 172 diamonds.\n Here, the Maison’s setters used an inverted setting meaning that the pointed end of the diamond faces out. These\n points are softened to make it comfortable to wear while adding a beautiful texture and harmonising with the Clous de Paris, another signature motif for Cartier.",
        "content2": "This exceptional version of the Baignoire watch is\n a manifesto in precious stones. 100 brilliant-cut\n diamonds are snow-set on the dial\n alone. On the case and the bangle, there are\n another 172 diamonds.\n Here, the Maison’s setters used an inverted setting,\n so the pointed end of the diamond faces out. These\n points are softened to make it comfortable to wear\n while adding a beautiful texture and harmonising\n with the Clous de Paris, another signature motif\n for Cartier.",
        "image": {
          "name": "baignoire-1"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "baignoire-1_mask.png",
          "width": 591,
          "height": 780
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_11.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "baignoire-2",
        "slug": "baignoire-2",
        "order": "05",
        "title": "Baignoire",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "With the case, bangle and dial fully covered with the\n Clous de Paris, this Baignoire created a change for\n the Maison’s polishers. This motif gives a sense of\n rhythm and a tactility to the watch, but it complicates\n what is already a meticulous process.\n The expert polishers involved in such a project need\n to be even more careful than usual to keep the exact\n shape and volume of each element throughout the\n process.\n It’s all done by hand, and it ensures the piece has\n maximal brilliance",
        "image": {
          "name": "baignoire-2"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "baignoire-2_mask.png",
          "width": 591,
          "height": 780
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_12.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "podcast",
        "uri": "myst-de-cartier",
        "slug": "myst-de-cartier",
        "order": "07",
        "title": "Myst de Cartier",
        "subtitle": "The Sound of Craft",
        "content": "The graphic black contrast of this Myst de Cartier\n is created with lacquer at the Maison des Métiers\n d’Art.\n This space, created by Cartier, is dedicated to rare\n techniques. It is located beside the watchmaking\n Manufacture to ensure close collaboration on\n special pieces.\n To achieve this effect, the lacquer artists must first\n laser-engrave the desired shapes and then fill the\n lacquer by hand on each one, before setting it at\n high temperatures.",
        "image": {
          "name": "myst-de-cartier"
        },
        "mask": {
          "url": "myst-de-cartier_mask.png",
          "width": 570,
          "height": 720
        },
        "audio": "/audio/podcasts/master_13.mp3",
        "loaded": 1
      }
    ]
  },
